WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.030
When Michigan Congressman Justin Amash

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:05.220
announced he was leaving the Republican Party
to become an independent,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.680
he didn’t just goodbye to the GOP--

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.080
he opened up a whole new set of possibilities
in American politics.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.520
It's gotten worse and worse and I think this
was the term that really broke it for me.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.200
In choosing principle over party,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.090
the 39-year-old son of a Palestinian refugee

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.800
has become the spokesman for all Americans
who believe in limited government.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.820
Since taking office in 2011,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.620
Amash has been an outspoken critic of out-of-control
government spending,

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.500
state surveillance, and unauthorized wars.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.520
He believes that Donald Trump engaged in impeachable
behavior,

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.260
but he’s primarily motivated by his belief

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:45.120
that Congress is no longer doing its job of
writing laws that the Executive branch implements.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.700
"The founders envisioned Congress as a deliberative
body in which outcomes are discovered,”

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.400
he wrote in the Washington Post.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.720
“We are fast approaching the point, however,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.980
where Congress exists as little more than
a formality to legitimize outcomes

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.520
dictated by the president, the speaker of
the House and the Senate majority leader.”

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.260
Amash doesn’t believe the system can be
reformed from within.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.380
I don't think there's anyone in there who
can change the system

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.840
It's pretty rigid.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.100
It's top-down.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.600
It comes down from leadership to the bottom.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.220
And over the years it's gotten more rigid.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.980
So it's more difficult now to change the process
than it was a few years ago.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:24.720
This is something Amash has been consistent
on for his entire time in Congress.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.100
He’s called out Nancy Pelosi

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.970
for straitjacketing the way legislation is
introduced, debated, and voted on in the house

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:35.970
while leveling exactly the same criticism
against her Republican predecessors.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.140
The speaker has not been protecting the institution.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.120
So you need a speaker in there who is an institutionalist,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.480
who cares about the institution first, who
is not a partisan.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.580
Let the house work it's will, let legislation
come to the floor,

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.020
let Republicans and Democrats and others offer
their amendments,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.180
and let's have votes on all sorts of things,
substantive things,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.100
not just post offices like they do now.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.260
Modern politics is “trapped in a partisan
death spiral,” he says.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.040
But there is a way out if Congress will actually
do its job.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.840
There is a desire for something else.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.600
I think the vast majority of Americans are
for that something else.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.480
And what you have right now are two parties
that are relatively small and weak,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.420
and, actually the reason they are so partisan
right now

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.290
is because they are small and weak.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.990
When you get parties that are that small,
that weak,

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:30.720
you start to have the fringier elements of
each party rise up and make the noise.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.420
Libertarian views are actually the mainstream
views.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.940
If you go around my district and talk to people,

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.349
most people have generally libertarian views.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:44.380
Now, would they call themselves libertarians,
would they say, "Hey, I'm a libertarian?"

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.540
Not necessarily,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.420
but they have a set of principles that are
American principles

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:54.140
embedded in this culture and this society,
and they are fairly libertarian principles.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.040
And I think we need to be able to present
that to them

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.950
and find some way to present an alternative
going forward.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.360
I think as these parties get smaller and smaller
there'll be more independents,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.740
and maybe a third party has a chance.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.380
But I think the future I see is one where
there are no strong parties

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.260
and more independent candidates.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.100
We don't really need the parties anymore.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.880
Amash says he will run for Congress as an
independent

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.000
and is confident that he can retain his seat.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.360
But he also hasn’t ruled out running for
president either, possibly as a Libertarian.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:27.430
Whether that happens, Justin Amash has already
stirred things up

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:31.989
by doing what he believes in rather than what
is convenient for partisan purposes.

00:03:31.989 --> 00:03:35.860
For that alone he deserves our attention and
commands our respect.

